    <h1>GDPR Article 27: The EU Representative Requirement Explained</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published September 19, 2026 &middot; Updated November 14, 2026</p>

    <p><strong>GDPR Article 27 requires a company that has no establishment in the EU, but is caught by the GDPR because it offers goods or services to people in the EU or monitors their behavior (Article 3(2)), to designate in writing a representative based in the EU.</strong> The representative is the local point of contact that EU supervisory authorities and individuals can address about your data processing. Only occasional, low-risk processing and public authorities are exempt, and failing to appoint one can be fined under Article 83(4)(a).</p>

    <p>This guide walks through Article 27 paragraph by paragraph, then covers who it applies to, what the representative actually does, how far a representative can be held liable, how to appoint one, and the clause you need in your privacy policy. If you are not yet sure the GDPR reaches your company at all, first read our explainer on <a href="/resources/does-gdpr-apply-to-us-companies/">how the GDPR's territorial scope works for US businesses</a>.</p>

    <div class="quick-check">
      <p class="quick-check-title">Quick check: do you need an Article 27 representative?</p>
      <p>You very likely need one if <strong>all</strong> of these are true:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your company has no office, branch, or other stable establishment in the EU.</li>
        <li>You offer an app, website, or service to people in the EU (free or paid), or you track their behavior online, for example with analytics or ad profiling.</li>
        <li>You process their personal data regularly rather than in a one-off, low-risk way.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You likely do <strong>not</strong> need one if you have a real EU establishment, if EU users reach you only incidentally and you do not target them, or if your processing meets every condition of the occasional-processing exemption described below.</p>
    </div>

