    <h2 id="article-27-text">The Text of Article 27, Paragraph by Paragraph</h2>

    <p>Article 27 is titled "Representatives of controllers or processors not established in the Union." It has five paragraphs. Here is what each one says and what it means in practice:</p>

    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="GDPR Article 27 paragraph by paragraph" tabindex="0">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Paragraph</th><th scope="col">What it says</th><th scope="col">What it means for you</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Art. 27(1)</th><td>"Where Article 3(2) applies, the controller or the processor shall designate in writing a representative in the Union."</td><td>If the GDPR applies to you only because you target or monitor people in the EU, you must appoint a representative, and the appointment must be written.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Art. 27(2)</th><td>The obligation does not apply to (a) processing that is occasional, does not include large-scale processing of special-category or criminal-offence data, and is unlikely to result in a risk to individuals; or (b) a public authority or body.</td><td>Two narrow exemptions. Most apps and websites with a steady flow of EU users do not fit either.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Art. 27(3)</th><td>The representative must be established in one of the member states where the data subjects whose data you process are located.</td><td>One representative in one member state covers the whole EU, as long as you have users or monitored individuals in that state.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Art. 27(4)</th><td>The representative is mandated to be addressed "in addition to or instead of" you by, in particular, supervisory authorities and data subjects, "on all issues related to processing."</td><td>Authorities and users can contact the representative instead of you, so the representative must be able to reach you and respond.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Art. 27(5)</th><td>Designating a representative is "without prejudice to legal actions which could be initiated against the controller or the processor themselves."</td><td>The representative is a contact point, not a liability shield. You remain responsible for compliance.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Recital 80 of the GDPR adds the intent behind the rule: the representative should act on behalf of the company and be the point of contact for supervisory authorities, and should be subject to enforcement proceedings if the company does not comply. Other articles then refer back to the representative: Articles 13(1)(a) and 14(1)(a) (it must be named in your privacy notice), Article 30 (records of processing), Article 31 (cooperation with authorities), and Article 58(1)(a) (authorities can order the representative to provide information).</p>

    <h2 id="who-must-appoint">Who Must Appoint an Article 27 Representative</h2>

    <p>Article 27 applies to both <strong>controllers</strong> (companies that decide why and how personal data is processed) and <strong>processors</strong> (companies that process it on a controller's behalf, such as a SaaS vendor hosting customer data). The trigger is Article 3(2), which brings a non-EU company under the GDPR when its processing relates to either:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Offering goods or services</strong> to people in the EU, whether or not payment is required (Art. 3(2)(a)). A free app counts.</li>
      <li><strong>Monitoring the behavior</strong> of people in the EU, as far as that behavior takes place in the EU (Art. 3(2)(b)). Tracking users across websites, behavioral advertising, and profiling-based analytics are the common examples.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>For the "offering" test, the European Data Protection Board's Guidelines 3/2018 on territorial scope look for signs that you intentionally target EU users, such as naming EU countries in your marketing, paying for ads aimed at EU audiences, using an EU country domain such as .de or .eu, offering EU languages or euro pricing that differ from your home market, listing EU phone numbers, or delivering goods to EU addresses. A US-only site that an EU visitor happens to find is not automatically "offering" services in the EU.</p>

    <p>One frequent trap: having an EU subsidiary does not automatically remove the obligation. If the subsidiary's activities are not linked to the processing in question, the non-EU parent can still fall under Article 3(2) and still need a representative for that processing.</p>

    <h2 id="exemptions">Article 27(2) Exemptions</h2>

    <p>There are only two exemptions, and both are narrower than most small companies expect.</p>

    <h3>Occasional, low-risk processing (Art. 27(2)(a))</h3>

    <p>All three conditions must be met at once:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The processing is <strong>occasional</strong>, not regular or systematic.</li>
      <li>It does not include <strong>large-scale</strong> processing of special-category data under Article 9(1) (health, biometric, religious, political, and similar data) or criminal-offence data under Article 10.</li>
      <li>It is <strong>unlikely to result in a risk</strong> to individuals' rights and freedoms, given its nature, context, scope, and purposes.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A US consultancy that once receives a short list of contacts from a single EU client could qualify. A mobile app that collects device identifiers and analytics from EU users every day does not, because that processing is continuous rather than occasional.</p>

    <h3>Public authorities and bodies (Art. 27(2)(b))</h3>

    <p>Government bodies are exempt. This never applies to private companies, startups, or independent developers.</p>

    <h2 id="duties">What an Article 27 Representative Does</h2>

    <p>The representative's role is mostly that of a local mailbox with a few concrete obligations of its own:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Receives and forwards communications.</strong> Supervisory authorities and data subjects can write to the representative about any processing issue, including access and deletion requests, and the representative passes them to you and helps you respond.</li>
      <li><strong>Keeps your records of processing.</strong> Article 30(1) and 30(2) require the representative to maintain the record of processing activities alongside you. You prepare the record; the representative holds it and produces it on request.</li>
      <li><strong>Cooperates with supervisory authorities.</strong> Article 31 applies to representatives, and Article 58(1)(a) lets an authority order the representative to provide information it needs for an investigation.</li>
      <li><strong>Communicates in the right language.</strong> The EDPB expects the representative to be able to communicate effectively with the authorities and individuals concerned, in principle in their own language.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>What the representative does <strong>not</strong> do: audit your practices, choose your lawful bases, run your data protection impact assessments, or answer for decisions you make. Those stay with you or your Data Protection Officer if you have one.</p>

    <h2 id="liability">Is the Representative Liable?</h2>

    <p>Article 27(5) keeps primary liability with you, but the boundaries of the representative's own exposure have been tested:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>The EDPB's position.</strong> Guidelines 3/2018 state that the representative is not directly liable for your obligations as controller or processor, but is liable for its own duties, such as keeping the Article 30 record and cooperating with authorities. The EDPB also notes that authorities can direct corrective measures and fines aimed at you through the representative, which is the enforcement purpose Recital 80 describes.</li>
      <li><strong>The courts.</strong> In <em>Sanso Rondon v LexisNexis Risk Solutions UK Ltd</em> [2021] EWHC 1427 (QB), the High Court of England and Wales held that an Article 27 representative could not be sued as a defendant for its client's alleged GDPR breaches. The ruling applied the UK-retained text, but it is the most detailed judicial reading of the article so far.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The practical takeaway: appointing a representative does not shift risk away from your company, and fines for your violations remain yours.</p>

    <h2 id="representative-vs-dpo">Article 27 Representative vs. DPO</h2>

    <p>The representative is often confused with a Data Protection Officer. They are separate roles with separate triggers:</p>

    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Article 27 representative compared with a Data Protection Officer" tabindex="0">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Article 27 representative</th><th scope="col">Data Protection Officer</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Legal basis</th><td>Article 27</td><td>Articles 37&ndash;39</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Trigger</th><td>No EU establishment and Article 3(2) applies</td><td>Public body, or core activities involving large-scale monitoring or large-scale special-category data</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Location</th><td>Must be established in an EU member state where your data subjects are</td><td>No fixed location, but must be easily accessible</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Function</th><td>External contact point acting on your mandate</td><td>Independent adviser who monitors your compliance</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Independence</th><td>Acts on your instructions</td><td>Must not receive instructions on its tasks (Art. 38(3))</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Because one acts on your instructions and the other must be independent, the EDPB considers the representative role incompatible with acting as the same company's external DPO. A company can need both, but it should give the roles to different people or providers.</p>

    <h2 id="how-to-appoint">How to Appoint a Representative</h2>

    <p>There is no registration with an EU authority. The appointment is a written mandate between you and the representative. A workable process looks like this:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Pick the member state.</strong> It must be one where your EU users or monitored individuals are. Many companies pick the country with their largest EU user base, or one whose language their team can work in.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose the representative.</strong> It can be an individual or an organization established in that state: a specialist representative provider, a law firm, a consultancy, or an affiliated company. Check that it can handle requests in the languages your users speak, hold your records securely, and reach you quickly.</li>
      <li><strong>Sign a written mandate.</strong> Name both parties, state that the designation is made under Article 27 GDPR, authorize the representative to be addressed by authorities and data subjects on all processing issues, require it to keep your Article 30 record and cooperate with authorities, and set out how requests will be forwarded, response times, term, and termination.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand over your records of processing.</strong> Purposes, categories of data and individuals, recipients, international transfers, retention periods, and security measures.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish the details.</strong> Add the representative's name and contact details to your privacy policy (Articles 13(1)(a) and 14(1)(a)) and keep them current if you change provider.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Once the representative is in place, the <a href="/tools/gdpr-compliance-checklist/">GDPR obligations checklist</a> is a quick way to confirm the rest of your setup, from lawful bases to data subject rights, is covered.</p>

    <h2 id="sample-clauses">Sample Article 27 Privacy Policy Clauses</h2>

    <p>Your privacy policy must identify your representative. Copy the clause below and replace the bracketed placeholders with your representative's details:</p>

    <div class="copy-block">
      <p class="copy-block-label">Sample clause: EU representative (GDPR Article 27)</p>
      <div class="copy-block-content">
        <p><strong>EU Representative</strong></p>
        <p>[Company Name] is not established in the European Union. In accordance with Article 27 of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), we have designated the following representative in the EU:</p>
        <p>[Representative Name]<br />[Street Address]<br />[Postal Code, City, Country]<br />Email: [representative@example.com]</p>
        <p>Our EU representative may be contacted, in addition to or instead of us, by data subjects and supervisory authorities on all issues relating to our processing of personal data. You can contact our representative to ask a question about this policy or to exercise your rights under the GDPR.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>If you also serve users in the United Kingdom, add a second block for your UK representative:</p>

    <div class="copy-block">
      <p class="copy-block-label">Sample clause: UK representative (UK GDPR Article 27)</p>
      <div class="copy-block-content">
        <p><strong>UK Representative</strong></p>
        <p>[Company Name] is not established in the United Kingdom. In accordance with Article 27 of the UK GDPR, we have designated the following representative in the UK:</p>
        <p>[Representative Name]<br />[Street Address]<br />[City, Postcode, United Kingdom]<br />Email: [uk-representative@example.com]</p>
        <p>Individuals in the UK and the Information Commissioner's Office may contact our UK representative on any matter relating to our processing of personal data.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Place these next to your own company identity and contact details, typically in a "Who we are" or "Contact us" section, so readers can find them without searching. If you are drafting the full policy from scratch, the <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">free privacy policy generator</a> structures the GDPR sections for you, and the <a href="/tools/privacy-law-checker/">privacy law checker</a> shows which other laws may apply.</p>

    <h2 id="uk-gdpr">UK GDPR Article 27</h2>

    <p>After Brexit the UK kept the GDPR as domestic law, including its own Article 27. If you are outside the UK and offer goods or services to people in the UK or monitor their behavior there, you need a <strong>separate representative established in the UK</strong>. An EU representative does not satisfy the UK rule, and a UK representative does not satisfy the EU rule. The same two exemptions apply, the counterpart regulator is the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO), and the UK fine ceiling for this tier is &pound;8.7 million or 2% of worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher.</p>

    <h2 id="penalties">Penalties and Enforcement</h2>

    <p>Article 83(4)(a) places the controller and processor obligations in Articles 25 to 39, which include Article 27, in the GDPR's lower fine tier: up to <strong>&euro;10 million or 2% of total worldwide annual turnover</strong> of the preceding financial year, whichever is higher. When setting the amount, authorities weigh the Article 83(2) factors, including how long the infringement lasted, whether it was negligent, and how well the company cooperated.</p>

    <p>Enforcement is not theoretical. In May 2021 the Dutch Data Protection Authority (Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens) fined Locatefamily.com &euro;525,000 for failing to designate an EU representative. The site published people's names, addresses, and phone numbers without any EU establishment, which left individuals with no practical way to reach it, and it had also failed to handle erasure requests. The authority also ordered it to appoint a representative by a set deadline, backed by a penalty for non-compliance. A missing representative also tends to surface during investigations into other complaints, where it adds a further infringement to the total.</p>

    <RelatedGuides currentSlug="resources/gdpr-eu-representative" />

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is Article 27 of the GDPR?</h3>
        <p>Article 27 is the GDPR provision that requires a controller or processor with no establishment in the EU, but subject to the GDPR under Article 3(2), to designate in writing a representative established in an EU member state. The representative is the contact point for supervisory authorities and individuals, and the obligation has narrow exemptions for occasional, low-risk processing and for public authorities.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is GDPR required in the USA?</h3>
        <p>The GDPR is EU law, but it applies to a US company that offers goods or services to people in the EU or monitors their behavior there. A US company in that position must comply with the GDPR and, unless an exemption applies, appoint an Article 27 representative in the EU. A US business that does not target or monitor people in the EU is generally outside its scope.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do I need an EU representative if my app is free?</h3>
        <p>Yes, if the app is offered to people in the EU and processes their personal data. Article 3(2)(a) covers offering goods or services "irrespective of whether a payment" is required, so a free app counts.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can the Article 27 representative be fined for my violations?</h3>
        <p>According to the EDPB, the representative is not directly liable for your obligations, only for its own, such as keeping the Article 30 record and cooperating with authorities. Authorities can still use the representative to direct corrective measures and fines at you. In Sanso Rondon v LexisNexis Risk Solutions UK Ltd (2021), the High Court of England and Wales held that a representative could not be sued for its client's breaches.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can my DPO also be my Article 27 representative?</h3>
        <p>The EDPB considers the two roles incompatible when the DPO is external: a DPO must act independently under Article 38(3), while a representative acts on your instructions. Give the roles to different people or providers.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Which EU country should my representative be in?</h3>
        <p>Any member state where people whose data you process are located. One representative covers the whole EU. Companies often pick the country with their largest EU user base or a language their team can work in.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can an EU customer or partner act as my representative?</h3>
        <p>Legally, any individual or organization established in a qualifying member state can be designated, provided the appointment is in writing. In practice the representative must keep your records, handle requests from authorities and users, and stay reachable, so a customer or business partner without that capacity is a weak choice and can create conflicts of interest.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do I need a separate representative for the UK?</h3>
        <p>Yes, if you target or monitor people in the UK. UK GDPR Article 27 requires a representative established in the UK, separate from your EU representative.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
